<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cîteaux Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1098</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cîteaux Abbey, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, was founded in 1098 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-of-molesme">Robert of Molesme</a> and a group of monks seeking a stricter observance of the Rule of Saint Benedict. It became the mother house of the Cistercian order, which spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-of-clairvaux">Bernard of Clairvaux</a>. Its monks built up the walled vineyard of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clos-de-vougeot">Clos de Vougeot</a>. Dissolved in the French Revolution, it was resettled by monks in 1898.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/citeaux-abbey.jpg" alt="Cîteaux Abbey" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: G CHP · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abbaye_de_C%C3%AEteaux_La_Biblioth%C3%A8que.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
